#!/usr/bin/python3
"""
titan-camera-gui: camera app for the Titan Pocket (GTK 4 + GStreamer, titan_ui4).

Live preview, photos, a front/rear switch and autofocus, on libcamera
(docs/camera.md): the libcamerasrc element runs the titan_camera driver
through libcamera's simple pipeline handler and software ISP (debayer on the
GPU, auto exposure, auto white balance).

- Preview: YUYV straight from MediaTek's pass-2 hardware ISP (the driver's
  YUYV node; rear 2336x1752, front 1632x1224), so the GPU only converts
  YUYV->RGB (glcolorconvert), never debayers. Rotated (and mirrored for the
  front camera) and scaled to about the screen's size on the GPU
  (glvideoflip, glcolorscale), then downloaded and shown as a texture. Set
  TITAN_CAMERA_HWISP=0 to fall back to the GPU software ISP (960x720 BGRx).
- Photo: the camera allows one stream at a time, so the shutter stops the
  preview, runs the camera at its full-resolution mode (rear 4672x3504,
  16 MP; front 3264x2448), skips the frames auto exposure needs to settle,
  saves one as JPEG under ~/Pictures/Camera and restarts the preview. With
  the hardware ISP the still is finished YUYV straight from the DIP (fast);
  with TITAN_CAMERA_HWISP=0 it is BGRx from the GPU software ISP (rear
  4668x3504, front 3260x2448, ~4 s).
- Rotation: the driver reports each sensor's mounting rotation
  (V4L2_CID_CAMERA_SENSOR_ROTATION); the rotate button overrides it per
  camera (~/.config/titan-camera/state.json), for photos too.
- Autofocus (rear camera; the front one is fixed-focus): contrast detection.
  A second branch of the preview pipeline hands each frame to an appsink;
  the gradient energy of the focus region (green channel) measures
  sharpness. The lens (AK7371, the sensor subdev's FOCUS_ABSOLUTE control;
  libcamera leaves it alone) sweeps coarsely until sharpness has clearly
  passed its peak, then finely around the best position. After a lens move
  only frames whose capture started after the move (sensor timestamps) are
  measured. Focused, the app watches sharpness and refocuses when it stays
  well below the focused level. Tap the preview to focus there; dragging the
  focus slider switches to manual focus until the next tap.
  On the software-ISP path (TITAN_CAMERA_HWISP=0) with the titan_camera
  driver's hardware statistics (/sys/.../rear/af_stats: MediaTek's ISP AF
  block, run beside the capture path), sharpness comes from them instead: the
  H0 filter energy of the focus region's blocks over their pixel sum, times
  the SGG1 gain the driver sets from the scene brightness, from frames at
  least AF_HW_SETTLE frames after the lens moved (the ISP's own frame
  counter). With the hardware ISP the preview frames themselves are measured:
  the statistics' filters see raw sensor noise and lose the focus peak in
  dim light. Flash metering uses the statistics either way.

- Flash (rear camera): a dual-tone pair on the MT6370 PMIC's two flash
  channels, channel 1 white (cool), channel 2 yellowish (warm), driven
  together through MediaTek's /dev/flashlight (flashlight-core +
  flashlights-mt6370). Modes: Off, Auto (fires when auto exposure has
  raised the sensor's analogue gain to 4x or more), On (both LEDs at the
  torch maximum, 400 mA each, for the whole photo capture: the still
  pipeline needs ~2 s for exposure to settle, longer than a strobe pulse;
  a 6 s hardware timeout backs it up), Torch (both at 175 mA while the app
  runs). The driver's default "coupled" scenario only switches anything on
  once both channels have a state, so both get a level and an on/off.
- Flash exposure (rear camera): libcamera's software AE starts a still
  capture from the dark-scene settings and moves 10-15 % a frame, so with
  the flash every frame used to be blown out. Instead the app meters with a
  pre-flash: the preview switches to a short manual exposure (gain 1x) with
  the flash on, and the hardware AF statistics (linear raw block sums,
  measured before any processing) show how bright the lit scene is, the
  exposure stepping down 8x while blocks clip and up 8x while the scene is
  dark; at the shortest exposure the pre-flash itself steps down 4x while
  blocks still clip (a very close subject). From that and the ambient
  statistics just before, the app picks the
  exposure time and gain that put the centre-weighted level at
  METER_TARGET with the brightest blocks below METER_HIGHLIGHT; when even
  the shortest exposure would be too bright (a close subject), the flash
  itself is dimmed. The still capture then starts with that exposure
  (libcamera manual exposure: our patch to the software ISP's AE) and the
  flash at that level, so its first frames are already right.
- AF assist: in Auto and On, when it's dark (the same 4x gain), both LEDs
  light at torch level while autofocus runs. Auto exposure gets
  AF_ASSIST_WAIT_MS to settle on the lit scene before the sweep starts; the
  light goes off when the lens has found focus. The focus is then held (no
  tracking, which would keep re-lighting in the dark) until a tap.

- QR codes: a third branch of the preview tee, right after libcamerasrc
  (before the GPU upload), takes QR_RATE frames a second through a leaky
  one-buffer queue, scales them to QR_SIZE grayscale and scans them with
  libzbar (ctypes) in that branch's own thread, so decoding never holds up
  the preview. zbar looks for QR codes only and reads every second line and
  column: ~14 ms a frame, against ~140 ms for GStreamer's zbar element,
  which tries every symbology on every line. Codes show as sticky chips
  over the bottom of the preview (up to QR_MAX, newest on top), outlined
  while the code is in view. A chip stays until its X is tapped. It shows
  what the code holds (link, Wi-Fi network, text) and offers Copy, and
  Open for web links: nothing is opened without a tap. A new chip comes
  with a short double buzz of the vibration motor (not repeated while the
  code stays in view).

Keys: Space/Enter take a photo, F switch camera, R rotate, A autofocus,
T flash mode.
"""
import ctypes
import fcntl
import json
import os
import re
import struct
import subprocess
import sys
import threading
import time
from urllib.parse import urlsplit

